The rules of this demonstration
01 / ENCOUNTER
Learn locally
Two simple reinforcement-style agents learn to associate symbols with hidden states.
02 / REPLACE
Lose the players
Both learners are erased at fixed intervals; the task remains unchanged.
03 / REMEMBER
Recover the protocol
Success traces seed the newcomers only when an archive is enabled.
04 / OBSERVE
Find the leak
A third learner can decode public feedback, but not private messages.
Why this is—and is not—interesting
This is a controlled toy-model proof of mechanism, not a claim of conscious AI, spontaneous general intelligence, a biological experiment, or novel scientific discovery. Its agents use simple numerical preference tables; the task is intentionally small. The result worth investigating is the tradeoff between cross-generation coordination and third-party inference, and whether it persists under tougher conditions.
Next experiments: replace actors asynchronously; introduce changing rules; let symbols acquire several meanings; hide or corrupt traces; measure counterfactual information leakage; compare against explicit codebooks and non-communicating baselines; test with independent LLM agents under strict authorization.
MELLON · PANTHEON
One board. Different games. A memory that outlives the players. A listener nobody invited.
Methods: 4 uniformly sampled hidden states, 4 symbol choices, ε-greedy learning (sender ε=0.22, receiver ε=0.12); after each round, sender receives success/failure feedback and receiver learns the true state. The persistent ledger records successful state–symbol pairings. Both active learners are reset each 240 rounds; ledger conditions initialize replacement learners from the record. Public condition exposes the symbol and eventual true-state feedback to an independent observer; private condition does not. All experiment arms use matched task randomness. For rigor, inspect the script and modify assumptions.
